  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant severe weather potential for \nTuesday...other chances for showers and storms Sunday and\nMonday. \n\nStratus clouds have finally begun to lift and scatter out- at\nleast west of the Blue Ridge, while to the east most ceilings \nremained at about a thousand feet or less. A surface trough \nbisected these two areas, with easterly winds bringing moisture \noff the ocean into the Piedmont. Aloft, much drier air was \nmoving in as seen on satellite imagery. Afternoon cumulus had \nreplaced the stratus in the mountains, so some sites were not \nseeing as much sun as others. Could see a few showers this \nafternoon and early evening, and perhaps an isolated \nthunderstorm along the surface trough. Cumulus clouds dissipate \nlater tonight, but stratus and fog creep back in after midnight.\n\n\nAlso overnight, a digging short wave will cause the upper flow\nto become more NNE as opposed to ENE, so any energy moving \ndown from the Great Lakes will cross the Blue Ridge. This will \ntrigger showers and thunderstorms Sunday, again on and around \nthe Blue Ridge. CAPE may reach a 1000 J/kg or better, especially\nalong the southern Blue Ridge, so a strong storm or two will be\npossible, with damaging winds the main concern. Heavy rain is \nexpected with these summertime storms, and up to an inch or \nmore can be expected in the stronger storms. Ironically one \nthing that will limit severity is the somewhat lower overall \nmoisture, with PWATs ranging from just over an inch to about an \ninch and a third. Monday looks even more anemic moisture-wise, \nso this should be a partly sunny day for most with no rain. \n\nMonday night surface flow will develop from the south, bringing \nincreasing dew points to the region. A warm front will lift \nacross the region overnight into early Tuesday morning, and this\nstrong WAA will bring widespread rain with embedded storms, a \nfew which may be strong. Later on Tuesday, a cold front moves \nthrough from north to south, and this, along with a developing \nlee trough ahead of it, would be the trigger for scattered \nsevere thunderstorms with wet microburst potential. This will \ndepend heavily on how fast we can recover from the morning \nconvection, and if we get breaks in cloud cover to recoup \ninstability. Deep layer shear looks to peak around 40 kts, and \ncurrent timing of the boundaries and diving short wave \nenergy/jet streak is around peak heating.\n\nWe are outlooked for both a 15% chance of severe weather from\nSPC on Day 4 (Tuesday), as well as a Marginal Risk of \nexcessive rainfall from WPC that day. UCAR's medium range \nconvective model continues to highlight Tuesday for a severe \nweather threat, consist of damaging winds and heavy rain \npossibly leading to flash flooding. \n\nConvection chances linger into Wednesday along the front, which\nmay stall in the vicinity of the NC/VA border. If so this area\ncould see a few strong storms. Cyclonic flow aloft will \ncontinue to threaten showers and storms north of it, but without\nthe same forcing as on Tuesday. After that, we should see a \nbreak from daily rain chances on Wednesday, with some isolated \nchances Friday and Saturday.",
